/// A value that is either a success or an error.
///
/// `T or E` is a builtin sum type: a success value of type `T` or an error
/// of type `E`. There are no `Ok`/`Err` constructors — bare values auto-wrap
/// to the correct branch at return, picked by the value's type
/// (type.errors/ER9). Disjointness rule: T ≠ E.
///
/// Every `E` must implement `ErrorMessage` (structural trait requiring
/// `message(self) -> string`; type.errors/ER4-6).
///
/// It has **no methods**. The operator surface is the whole API:
///
///     r is E as e          did it fail, and with what?   [ER23]
///     try r                or leave, error unchanged     [ER16]
///     r catch e => v       handle it, naming the error   [ER14]
///     r catch _ => v       handle it, dropping it visibly
///     r catch e => return f(e)   transform and leave
///     r catch _ => none    drop the error, keep a `T?`
///     r!                   or panic
///     match r { … }        when there are more than two outcomes
///
/// An error is never dropped without the source saying so — that's why
/// `catch`'s binder is mandatory and there is no bare-value form.
public enum Result<T, E> { }
